# Development-only files, kept out of the packed mod.
#
# Entries are exact relative paths -- modkit matches the whole path, not a
# directory prefix -- so a new test file needs its own line here.
#
# `modkit pack` zips everything under the mod directory, so anything listed
# here is also left out of the release, which is what we want: the suites need
# a gen1recomp checkout to run against and are no part of the installed mod.
# It is also what keeps `gen2check`'s module-reach notes about them out of the
# gate output, since a test that never runs in the game has no Gen 2
# reachability to report.  See README, "Verifying it".
#
# A dot-directory is skipped wholesale by both packers, which is why .probe/
# is not listed here -- but see .gitignore, which does list it, because
# publishing the reverse-engineering evidence is a different question from
# packaging it.
tests/celebi_event_test.lua
tests/launcher_update_test.lua

# tools/ is the same kind of thing: build_release.py is what CI runs to make
# the release zip, and author_celebi_sheet.py re-derives the shipped sheet from
# a cart's own copy of it (see assets/README.md).  Both are authoring-side; an
# installed mod has no use for either.
#
# assets/ is NOT listed here, and that is the point: assets/celebi.png SHIPS.
# It is a re-cut of the cart's own overworld graphic and so Nintendo's, but
# nothing can derive it at runtime -- Gold has no Celebi overworld sprite, so
# the engine's extractor never writes one -- which makes it a choice between
# carrying the file and shipping a descent with no sprite in it.  Versions
# 1.4.5 and 1.4.6 took the second road; 1.4.7 carries it.  assets/README.md
# records the whole trade.
tools/build_release.py
tools/author_celebi_sheet.py

# A guard, not a description of the tree.
#
# The tree itself carries no build output: the release zip is built by CI from
# the tagged tree and attached to the GitHub Release, never committed (see
# .github/workflows/release.yml, and .gitignore).  These two lines are here for
# the case where somebody builds a release by hand *into the repo root* -- both
# `modkit pack` and tools/build_release.py walk the whole tree, so a stray
# artifact gets folded into the next build.
#
# Exact paths, like every other entry here, so a version bump means renaming
# them.  `tests/launcher_update_test.lua` fails if they stop matching the
# manifest's id and version, which is the reminder.
#
# Note the underscore: these are named after the manifest id, `celebi_event`,
# not after the repo or the mod folder, which use a hyphen.  The launcher
# matches the release asset against the id exactly, so the id spelling is the
# one that matters.
celebi_event-1.4.8.modpkg
celebi_event-1.4.8.zip

# RELEASE_NOTES.md is the release BODY, not a file the installed mod reads:
# .github/workflows/release.yml hands it to `gh release create --notes-file`,
# and the launcher renders it in the "What's New?" modal.  Authoring-side, so
# it stays out of the package like the tests and tools above.  It is committed
# (see .gitignore) because the workflow needs it at the tagged commit.
RELEASE_NOTES.md
